SYSTEMVERILOG - ADVANCED FEATURES & FUNCTIONS
==============================================

SystemVerilog is an extension of Verilog that adds many advanced features for 
verification, modeling, and design. Here are the key additional features:


1. DATA TYPES
=============

1.1 LOGIC TYPE
--------------
Syntax: logic [range] name;
Explanation: SystemVerilog's improved version of "reg" and "wire". Can be driven 
by both continuous assignment and procedural code. Resolves issues with 4-state 
logic ('0', '1', 'x', 'z').
Example:
  logic [7:0] data;        // 8-bit logic
  logic [3:0] counter;     // 4-bit logic


1.2 PACKED AND UNPACKED ARRAYS
-------------------------------
Syntax: 
  logic [7:0][3:0] packed_array;      // Packed array
  integer unpacked_array[0:9];         // Unpacked array

Explanation: Packed arrays are part of the data type and can be treated as a single 
value. Unpacked arrays are separate elements. Packed arrays are useful for bit 
operations.
Example:
  logic [7:0][3:0] mem;               // 8 groups of 4 bits
  logic data_array[0:3][0:3];         // 4x4 unpacked array


1.3 STRUCTURES AND UNIONS
--------------------------
Syntax:
  struct {
    int id;
    string name;
    bit [7:0] value;
  } my_struct;
  
Explanation: Allows grouping related data of different types. Unions are similar 
but share memory between members. Very useful for organizing complex data.
Example:
  struct {
    bit [31:0] address;
    bit [31:0] data;
    bit [3:0] control;
  } transaction;


1.4 ENUMERATION
---------------
Syntax:
  enum {RED, GREEN, BLUE} color;
  
Explanation: Defines a set of named integer constants. Makes code more readable 
and maintainable. Can specify the underlying data type.
Example:
  enum bit [1:0] {IDLE=2'b00, ACTIVE=2'b01, DONE=2'b10} state;


1.5 USER-DEFINED TYPES (TYPEDEF)
---------------------------------
Syntax:
  typedef struct {
    int a;
    string b;
  } my_type;

Explanation: Creates a new data type that can be reused throughout code. Reduces 
code duplication and improves readability.
Example:
  typedef logic [31:0] word_t;
  word_t instruction;


2. CLASSES AND OBJECT-ORIENTED PROGRAMMING
============================================

2.1 CLASS DEFINITION
--------------------
Syntax:
  class ClassName;
    // Properties
    int value;
    // Methods
    function int get_value();
      return value;
    endfunction
  endclass

Explanation: Classes allow encapsulation of data and methods. Essential for 
verification environments. Supports inheritance, polymorphism, and encapsulation.
Example:
  class Transaction;
    rand bit [31:0] address;
    rand bit [31:0] data;
    bit write_enable;
  endclass


2.2 INHERITANCE
---------------
Syntax:
  class Derived extends Base;
    // Additional properties and methods
  endclass

Explanation: Allows a class to inherit properties and methods from a parent class. 
Enables code reuse and creates class hierarchies.
Example:
  class MemoryTransaction extends Transaction;
    bit [3:0] memory_type;
  endclass


2.3 CONSTRUCTORS
----------------
Syntax:
  class MyClass;
    function new(int val);
      value = val;
    endfunction
  endclass

Explanation: Special method called when creating an object. Initializes object 
properties and allocates memory.
Example:
  MyClass obj = new(10);


2.4 POLYMORPHISM AND VIRTUAL METHODS
-------------------------------------
Syntax:
  class Base;
    virtual function void display();
      $display("Base");
    endfunction
  endclass
  
  class Derived extends Base;
    function void display();
      $display("Derived");
    endfunction
  endclass

Explanation: Virtual functions can be overridden in derived classes. Enables 
different behavior based on object type.
Example:
  Base b = new Derived();
  b.display();  // Prints "Derived"


3. RANDOMIZATION
================

3.1 RANDOM VARIABLES
--------------------
Syntax:
  class Transaction;
    rand bit [31:0] addr;
    rand bit [7:0] data;
    randc bit [3:0] priority;  // cyclic (cycles through values)
  endclass

Explanation: "rand" makes variables randomizable. "randc" is cyclic randomization. 
Critical for testbench verification and corner case testing.
Example:
  Transaction txn = new();
  txn.randomize();  // Generate random values


3.2 RANDOMIZE WITH CONSTRAINTS
-------------------------------
Syntax:
  class MyClass;
    rand int value;
    constraint range { value inside {[0:100]}; }
  endclass

Explanation: Constraints limit the range of random values. Enables targeted testing 
of specific scenarios.
Example:
  class Request;
    rand bit [7:0] addr;
    rand bit [31:0] data;
    constraint valid_addr { addr > 0; addr < 256; }
  endclass


3.3 CONSTRAINT BLOCKS
---------------------
Syntax:
  if (txn.randomize() with { addr inside {[0:1023]}; }) begin
    // Randomization succeeded
  end

Explanation: Inline constraints for one-time use. More flexible than class-level 
constraints.
Example:
  txn.randomize() with { data == 32'hDEADBEEF; };


4. INTERFACES
=============

4.1 INTERFACE DEFINITION
------------------------
Syntax:
  interface BusInterface(input clk);
    logic [31:0] address;
    logic [31:0] data;
    logic write_enable;
    logic read_enable;
  endinterface

Explanation: Groups related signals together into a reusable bundle. Simplifies 
port connections and improves modularity. Essential for testbenches.
Example:
  interface APB(input clk);
    logic [31:0] paddr;
    logic [31:0] pwdata;
    logic pwrite;
    logic psel;
    logic penable;
  endinterface


4.2 MODPORTS
-----------
Syntax:
  interface BusInterface;
    logic [31:0] address;
    logic [31:0] data;
    
    modport Master (
      output address,
      output data
    );
    
    modport Slave (
      input address,
      input data
    );
  endinterface

Explanation: Defines different viewpoints of an interface. Master sees outputs, 
Slave sees inputs. Prevents accidental connections and improves type checking.


4.3 INTERFACE IN MODULES
------------------------
Syntax:
  module MyModule(BusInterface bus);
    always @(posedge bus.clk) begin
      // Use bus.address, bus.data, etc.
    end
  endmodule

Explanation: Pass entire interface to modules instead of individual signals. 
Reduces port declarations and improves readability.


5. ASSERTIONS
=============

5.1 CONCURRENT ASSERTIONS
---------------------------
Syntax:
  assert property (@(posedge clk) signal == 1'b1);

Explanation: Checks properties continuously during simulation. Monitors protocol 
compliance and design correctness.
Example:
  assert property (@(posedge clk) (req |-> ack) );
  // If req is high, ack must be high next cycle


5.2 SEQUENCE DEFINITION
-----------------------
Syntax:
  sequence read_sequence;
    (read_en & ~write_en) ##1 (data_valid);
  endsequence

Explanation: Defines temporal sequences of events. ## specifies clock cycles between 
events. Useful for complex protocol verification.
Example:
  sequence valid_transaction;
    (valid & ready) ##1 (~valid);
  endsequence


5.3 PROPERTY DEFINITION
-----------------------
Syntax:
  property valid_write;
    @(posedge clk)
    (write_en) |-> (data_valid);
  endproperty

Explanation: Combines sequences into verifiable properties. |-> means "implies". 
Enables formal verification.


6. PROCEDURAL BLOCKS
====================

6.1 ALWAYS_COMB
---------------
Syntax:
  always_comb begin
    output = input1 & input2;
  end

Explanation: Replaces "always @(*)". Automatically triggers on input changes. 
Better for combinational logic, provides error checking.
Example:
  always_comb
    result = a + b + carry;


6.2 ALWAYS_FF
-------------
Syntax:
  always_ff @(posedge clk or negedge reset) begin
    if (!reset)
      q <= 1'b0;
    else
      q <= d;
  end

Explanation: Specifically for sequential logic triggered by clock edge. Makes intent 
clear and catches synthesis errors.


6.3 ALWAYS_LATCH
----------------
Syntax:
  always_latch begin
    if (enable)
      q = d;
  end

Explanation: For latches (combinational logic with feedback). Rare, but explicit 
when needed.


7. FUNCTIONS AND TASKS
======================

7.1 FUNCTIONS
-------------
Syntax:
  function int add(int a, int b);
    return a + b;
  endfunction

Explanation: Similar to Verilog but with better type support and no delays. Returns 
a value.
Example:
  function bit [31:0] reverse_bits(bit [31:0] data);
    for (int i=0; i<32; i++)
      reverse_bits[i] = data[31-i];
  endfunction


7.2 TASKS
---------
Syntax:
  task automatic process_data(input int data, output int result);
    // Can have delays
    #10;
    result = data * 2;
  endtask

Explanation: Can contain delays and multiple statements. "automatic" allows 
recursion. No return value (uses output arguments).
Example:
  task automatic calculate(input [31:0] x, output [31:0] y);
    y = x + 1;
    #5 y = x * 2;
  endtask


8. GENERATE BLOCKS
==================

8.1 GENERATE FOR LOOP
---------------------
Syntax:
  generate
    for (genvar i=0; i<8; i++) begin
      assign data_out[i] = data_in[i] & enable;
    end
  endgenerate

Explanation: Creates multiple instances of logic/modules at compile time. Enables 
parameterizable designs.
Example:
  generate
    for (genvar i=0; i<WIDTH; i++) begin
      and_gate gate_i (
        .in1(a[i]),
        .in2(b[i]),
        .out(c[i])
      );
    end
  endgenerate


8.2 GENERATE IF
---------------
Syntax:
  generate
    if (USE_PARITY == 1) begin
      parity_checker pcheck(...);
    end
  endgenerate

Explanation: Conditionally generates blocks at compile time. Useful for design 
variants.


9. STRING AND PRINTING
======================

9.1 STRING TYPE
---------------
Syntax:
  string name = "SystemVerilog";

Explanation: Built-in string type (not available in Verilog). Can be manipulated 
with functions.
Example:
  string msg = "Hello";
  msg = {msg, " World"};  // Concatenation


9.2 STRING FUNCTIONS
--------------------
Syntax:
  len = name.len();           // Get length
  name = name.toupper();      // Convert to uppercase
  substr = name.substr(0, 4); // Get substring

Explanation: Built-in string methods for manipulation and analysis.


9.3 FORMATTED PRINTING
----------------------
Syntax:
  $display("Value: %d, String: %s", value, name);
  $displayh("Hex: %h", data);
  $displayo("Octal: %o", data);

Explanation: Enhanced display functions. More formatting options than Verilog.
Example:
  $display("Address: %08h, Data: %08h", addr, data);


10. KEYWORDS AND OPERATORS
===========================

10.1 LOGIC EQUALITY OPERATORS
------------------------------
  === and !==  → Equality with x/z matching
  == and !=    → Logical equality (x/z treated as false)

Explanation: === checks if values are exactly equal including x and z. == performs 
logical comparison.
Example:
  if (value === 32'hXXXXXXXX) // True if all bits are x
  if (value == 32'h00000000)  // True if equal


10.2 INSIDE OPERATOR
---------------------
Syntax:
  if (value inside {[0:100], 200, 300})
    // value is in range 0-100 or equals 200 or 300

Explanation: Checks if value is inside a set or range. Very useful for constraints 
and conditions.
Example:
  if (addr inside {[0:0xFFFF]}) // Valid address range


10.3 WILDCARD COMPARISON
------------------------
Syntax:
  if (addr inside {32'h1000_???? })  // ???? matches any bits
    valid = 1;

Explanation: Uses wildcards for pattern matching. ? matches any bit.


11. RANDOMIZATION FEATURES
===========================

11.1 CONSTRAINT OPERATORS
--------------------------
  inside        → Value within set/range
  dist          → Distribution of random values
  ->            → Implication
  unique        → All values must be unique
  if-else       → Conditional constraints

Example:
  constraint priority_dist {
    priority dist {1 := 70, 2 := 20, 3 := 10};
  }


11.2 COVERAGE COLLECTION
------------------------
Syntax:
  covergroup cg @(posedge clk);
    cp_addr: coverpoint addr {
      bins low = {[0:255]};
      bins high = {[256:511]};
    }
  endgroup

Explanation: Collects coverage metrics during simulation. Verifies test completeness.


12. DIRECTIVES AND COMPILER
============================

12.1 TIMESCALE DIRECTIVE
------------------------
Syntax: `timescale 1ns / 1ps

Explanation: Sets simulation time unit and resolution. ns = nanosecond, ps = picosecond.


12.2 DEFINE DIRECTIVE
---------------------
Syntax: `define MAX_ADDR 32'hFFFF

Explanation: Preprocessor macro for compile-time constants.


12.3 IFDEF DIRECTIVE
---------------------
Syntax:
  `ifdef DEBUG
    $display("Debug message");
  `endif

Explanation: Conditional compilation. Useful for debug vs. production builds.


COMPARISON: VERILOG vs SYSTEMVERILOG
======================================

Feature                     | Verilog | SystemVerilog
----------------------------|---------|---------------
Classes                     |    No   |      Yes
Interfaces                  |    No   |      Yes
Assertions                  |    No   |      Yes
String Type                 |    No   |      Yes
Structures                  |    No   |      Yes
Enumerations                |    No   |      Yes
Dynamic Arrays              |    No   |      Yes
Randomization               |    No   |      Yes
always_comb/always_ff       |    No   |      Yes
Improved Type System        |    No   |      Yes
Generate Blocks             |Limited  |   Full
Functions                   |Limited  |   Full


COMMON SYSTEMVERILOG TESTBENCH STRUCTURE
==========================================

module tb;
  // 1. Interface
  BusInterface bus(clk);
  
  // 2. DUT (Device Under Test)
  DUT dut(.bus(bus));
  
  // 3. Clock Generation
  always #5 clk = ~clk;
  
  // 4. Testbench Class
  class Testbench;
    task run();
      // Generate transactions
      // Send to DUT
      // Check responses
    endtask
  endclass
  
  // 5. Initial Block
  initial begin
    Testbench tb = new();
    tb.run();
    $finish;
  end
endmodule

